wasix-libc: an exiting thread must not touch its stack after it releases the
thread-list lock (Infrawrench/Infrawrench#967).

Against wasix-org/wasix-libc 67b2eccf (v2026-07-30.1); the code is the same on
main at 2025b44a (v2026-10-02.1). Ours: not yet reported or fixed upstream.

__pthread_exit releases the thread-list lock itself, which is what lets
pthread_join free the thread's stack, TLS and thread structure. It did so with
__tl_unlock, whose wake of the lock's waiters is the host call futex_wake, and
the host writes that call's result (one byte) through a pointer into the
exiting thread's stack after it has woken the waiter. The joiner can free the
block, and another pthread_create be handed it, before that write lands.

diff --git a/libc-top-half/musl/src/thread/pthread_create.c b/libc-top-half/musl/src/thread/pthread_create.c
index 9d668be..92c5cc1 100644
--- a/libc-top-half/musl/src/thread/pthread_create.c
+++ b/libc-top-half/musl/src/thread/pthread_create.c
@@ -201,9 +201,25 @@ _Noreturn void __pthread_exit(void *result)
 #ifdef __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream
 	for (;;) __syscall(SYS_exit, 0);
 #else
-	// __syscall(SYS_exit) would unlock the thread, list
-	// do it manually here
-	__tl_unlock();
+	// __syscall(SYS_exit) would unlock the thread list, do it manually here.
+	//
+	// Once the lock is released, pthread_join frees this thread's stack, TLS
+	// and thread structure, and another pthread_create can be handed the same
+	// block at once. The kernel releases the lock for upstream musl after the
+	// thread is gone; here the thread is still running, so nothing from the
+	// store on may touch that block. That rules out __tl_unlock: its wake is a
+	// host call (futex_wake) that writes its result through a pointer into
+	// this stack, after it has woken the waiter. The result goes to static
+	// storage instead; nobody reads it. The lock is held exactly once here
+	// (taken above, and never by a thread that already holds it), so there is
+	// no count to unwind.
+	static __wasi_bool_t exit_woken;
+	a_store(&__thread_list_lock, 0);
+	if (tl_lock_waiters) {
+		// A failed wake has nowhere to be reported from here.
+		__wasi_errno_t ignored = __wasi_futex_wake((uint32_t *)&__thread_list_lock, &exit_woken);
+		(void)ignored;
+	}
 	for (;;) __wasi_thread_exit(0);
 #endif
 }
